---

### Possible Implementation

Here’s a practical way to wire **@opentui/core + nspell** so you can **highlight misspelled words as the user types**—without fighting the fact that OpenTUI’s `Input` itself isn’t designed for per-span styling.

OpenTUI gives you:

* `InputRenderable` + `InputRenderableEvents.INPUT` to react on each keystroke ([[opentui.com](https://opentui.com/docs/components/input/)][1])
* `TextRenderable` + `t` template literal + helpers like `fg()`, `underline()`, etc. for rich, inline styling ([[opentui.com](https://opentui.com/docs/components/text/)][2])
  nspell gives you:
* `correct(word)` and `suggest(word)` ([[npm](https://www.npmjs.com/package/nspell)][3])
  and `dictionary-en` is a ready-to-use Hunspell dictionary commonly used with nspell ([[GitHub](https://github.com/wooorm/dictionaries)][4])

---

## Integration approach (low-disruption)

Because the input widget itself can’t style individual substrings, the most ergonomic pattern is:

1. **Keep `Input` normal** (fast, stable cursor behavior).
2. Render a **“styled mirror line”** beneath (or above) the input that:

   * repeats the same text
   * underlines / colors only the misspelled words

This avoids cursor jumps and keeps typing smooth.

Other UX-friendly highlight options (pick one):

* **Underline-only** misspellings (most subtle; best default).
* **Dim red underline** (still subtle, more noticeable).
* **Background tint** (stronger, but can feel noisy in terminals).
* **Inline markers** (e.g., “~” under the word on a second guide line) if you want *zero* color reliance.
* **Right-side hint panel** showing the *first* suggestion for the word under/near the cursor (less visual clutter).

---

## Small sample (TypeScript / ESM)

This example:

* creates an input
* on each keystroke, tokenizes into words/spaces
* uses `nspell.correct()` to detect mistakes
* renders a styled preview line with underlined red misspellings via OpenTUI’s `TextRenderable` + `t` ([[opentui.com](https://opentui.com/docs/components/text/)][2])

```ts
import {
  createCliRenderer,
  Box,
  Input,
  Text,
  TextRenderable,
  InputRenderableEvents,
  t,
  fg,
  underline,
  dim,
} from "@opentui/core";

import nspell from "nspell";
import dictionary from "dictionary-en";

// Simple tokenizer that preserves whitespace as tokens
function tokenizePreserveSpaces(value: string): string[] {
  // splits into runs of letters/apostrophes or runs of whitespace or single non-space symbols
  // good enough for a demo; tune for your domain
  return value.match(/[A-Za-z']+|\s+|[^\sA-Za-z']+/g) ?? [value];
}

function isWordToken(token: string) {
  return /^[A-Za-z']+$/.test(token);
}

